"""Offline transport lesson; all probabilities are calculated by Python/SDK."""
import json


def render_glued_trees(data):
    payload = json.dumps(data,allow_nan=False).replace('&','\\u0026').replace('<','\\u003c').replace('>','\\u003e')
    return _PAGE.replace('__DATA__',payload)


_PAGE = r'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>NexQus · Glued-tree quantum transport</title><style>
*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#eef3fa;color:#1b304d;font:15px/1.55 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:1240px;margin:auto;padding:26px}h1{font-size:clamp(27px,4vw,42px);line-height:1.2;margin:6px 0 15px}h2{font-size:21px;margin:0 0 12px}p{margin:8px 0 13px}.card{padding:23px;background:white;border:1px solid #d7e1ed;border-radius:16px;margin:18px 0}.small{font-size:13px;color:#52677e}.notice{background:#fff5de;border-left:4px solid #d2941d;padding:13px}.row{display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:20px}.grid .card{margin:0}.scroll{overflow:auto}svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}button,select{font:inherit;padding:8px 13px;border:1px solid #9baec5;border-radius:8px;background:white;color:#1b304d}button{cursor:pointer}button:disabled{opacity:.4}button:focus-visible,input:focus-visible,select:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #dca02f}input[type=range]{width:100%;accent-color:#2859d6}.metrics{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:10px;margin:13px 0}.metric{padding:13px;background:#eff5fb;border-radius:10px}.metric strong{display:block;font-size:23px}.metric span{font-size:12px;color:#52677e}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:12px}th,td{padding:9px;border-bottom:1px solid #dae4ef;text-align:left}th{background:#f0f5fa}pre{font-size:12px;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}#circuit svg{min-width:500px}a{color:#2859d6}@media(max-width:780px){main{padding:14px}.card{padding:16px}.grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.metrics{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style></head><body><main><p class="small">NexQus school lab · continuous-time quantum walk · local simulation</p><h1>Quantum transport through glued binary trees</h1>
<p>The random-cycle glued-tree family supports a proven exponential quantum query advantage under hidden-name neighbor-oracle access. This lesson reproduces its transport dynamics using known column symmetry, and checks small full graphs independently.</p>
<div class="notice">We simulate 2n+2 column states, not every vertex of the exponentially large graph. Both quantum and classical models exploit this reduction. The circuit below acts on column indices; it does not implement the paper's coherent hidden-name oracle. CPU speed differences here are numerical implementation differences, not QPU advantage.</div>
<div class="card"><h2>The graph that matters</h2><p class="small">Two complete binary trees of depth n. Their leaves are connected by one random alternating cycle (purple), not merged. All vertices have degree 3 except the entrance and exit, which have degree 2. The drawing reveals structure for explanation; the neighbor-query solver receives only opaque random vertex names.</p>
<svg id="graph" viewBox="0 0 1000 360" role="img" aria-label="Explicit depth-four random-cycle glued binary trees"></svg><p id="graph-info" class="small"></p><pre id="node-info">Click a vertex to inspect its hidden name and neighbor names.</pre></div>
<div class="card"><h2>How the walk works</h2><ol><li><strong>Prepare the entrance.</strong> There is no coin register and no global Grover route oracle. The graph adjacency defines H=γA, with γ=1/√2 per edge.</li><li><strong>Evolve coherently.</strong> ψ(t)=exp(−iHt)ψ(0). Each amplitude's derivative is −iγ times the sum of neighboring amplitudes. Phase differences create interference.</li><li><strong>Use symmetry to inspect it.</strong> A column state is the equal-amplitude sum over all vertices at that level, normalized by √(column size). Couplings between columns are 1, except √2 across the weld. Equal amplitudes per vertex remain equal in a column even though the weld is random.</li><li><strong>Choose time before measuring.</strong> Reset, choose t uniformly from [0,T] where T=4(n+1), then measure once. The main success statistic is the analytic average probability of observing the exit. A failed attempt may be repeated. This short transport window is an experiment, not the conservative polynomial-time bound from the theorem.</li><li><strong>Classical comparison.</strong> A continuous-time random walker jumps along each incident edge at rate γ. At each column, aggregate all probability mass. On the exit side there are twice as many edges leading back toward the weld as toward the exit, so classical transport is biased away from the exit.</li></ol>
<p class="small">On the hidden graph, the quantum dynamics do not need to know which vertex belongs to which column: the symmetry emerges from the evolution. Our CPU model uses the column assignment as an analyst's shortcut. That assignment is not supplied to an algorithm in the oracle theorem.</p>
<p id="protocol" class="small"></p><p class="notice">The exit is not absorbing. Curves show probability at the exit at a single final measurement time, not “ever reached the exit.” Moving the slider inspects independent experiments; it does not measure the walker along the way. Finding the exit name also does not return a route.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="row"><label>Tree depth <select id="depth"></select></label><button id="back">Back</button><button id="next">Next time</button><button id="play">Play</button><strong id="time"></strong></div><input id="time-range" type="range" min="0" value="0" aria-label="Evolution time">
<div class="metrics"><div class="metric"><strong id="vertices"></strong><span>Implied full-graph vertices</span></div><div class="metric"><strong id="columns"></strong><span>Column states actually simulated</span></div><div class="metric"><strong id="register"></strong><span>Column-register qubits (not oracle-name bits)</span></div></div><p id="encoding" class="small"></p></div>
<div class="grid"><div class="card"><h2>Quantum column probability</h2><svg id="qbars" viewBox="0 0 550 230" role="img" aria-label="Quantum probability by column"></svg></div><div class="card"><h2>Classical column probability</h2><svg id="cbars" viewBox="0 0 550 230" role="img" aria-label="Classical probability by column"></svg></div></div><p class="small">Fixed 0–100% vertical scales; bars are total column probabilities, not probability of each individual vertex. Blue: quantum; orange: classical. Click a column.</p>
<div class="card"><h2>Inspect amplitude and interference</h2><pre id="amplitude"></pre><p class="small">The probability of an individual vertex is column probability divided by column size. The local derivative explains amplitude flow; amplitude itself can be complex. Probability is |a|², not a.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>Exit probability at each final time</h2><svg id="exit-chart" viewBox="0 0 900 270" role="img" aria-label="Exit occupancy versus model time"></svg><p id="exit-info"></p><p class="small">Blue: quantum; orange: classical. The grid maximum is descriptive only. The reported protocol chooses time uniformly and does not choose the best-looking time.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>Depth sweep: the widening transport gap</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Depth n</th><th>Implied vertices</th><th>Simulated columns</th><th>Quantum uniform-time exit</th><th>Classical uniform-time exit</th><th>Q/C probability ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody id="sweep"></tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">All requested depths are reported. The quantum average is an analytic time integral; the classical average uses a positive Poisson expansion, retaining tiny probabilities without subtracting nearly equal numbers. No seed or peak-time selection is used to improve these values.</p>
<svg id="scaling" viewBox="0 0 900 280" role="img" aria-label="Expected model time on a logarithmic scale versus depth"></svg><p class="small">Expected model time, logarithmic scale: blue = repeated quantum uniform-time attempts T/(2p), orange = classical random walk mean first-passage time. Both use the same γ. Quantum repeats assume free reset and exit verification; neither curve is hardware runtime or the optimal classical algorithm's query complexity. At small depths the quantum protocol may cost more.</p>
<p class="notice">The exponential separation against all classical algorithms is a theorem about hidden random names and local neighbor access, not a conclusion drawn from these two curves. Revealing columns makes this family easy to simulate classically too. BFS with the full graph is not the same input model as a hidden oracle.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>Independent full-graph checks</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th>Explicit vertices</th><th>H B − B Hcolumn error</th><th>Full-state error</th><th>BFS neighbor queries</th></tr></thead><tbody id="verification"></tbody></table><p class="small">B maps normalized columns to full-vertex amplitudes. Full-graph spectral evolution is compared with SDK column-circuit output at five times. The neighbor-query BFS identifies the other degree-2 vertex without receiving its name or tree coordinates. Testing this one classical strategy is not a classical lower-bound proof.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>Actual SDK circuit and execution costs</h2><div id="circuit" class="scroll"></div><p class="small">Column-to-energy basis change, energy-dependent phase evolution, and inverse basis change. Three logical operations on the reduced register; no hardware-native gate count or coherent graph-oracle implementation is claimed.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th>Quantum model wall ms</th><th>Classical-model CPU ms</th></tr></thead><tbody id="timings"></tbody></table><p id="timing-scope" class="small"></p></div>
<div class="card"><h2>Research context</h2><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0209131">Childs, Cleve, Deotto, Farhi, Gutmann and Spielman, STOC 2003</a>: Exponential algorithmic speedup by quantum walk. Sections II and III distinguish the simple leaf-identified graph from the random-cycle construction and derive its column Hamiltonian. Section IV gives the classical oracle lower bound.</p><p class="small">This is a transport reproduction and explanation. It does not run on a QPU, materialize the large graphs, implement the hidden-name coherent oracle, or independently prove the theorem.</p></div>
</main><script id="glued-data" type="application/json">__DATA__</script><script>
'use strict';const data=JSON.parse(document.getElementById('glued-data').textContent),$=id=>document.getElementById(id),ns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';let index=0,frame=0,column=0,timer=null;const pct=v=>v===0?'0%':v<.00001?(100*v).toExponential(3)+'%':(100*v).toFixed(4)+'%';function el(tag,attrs,text){const e=document.createElementNS(ns,tag);for(const [k,v] of Object.entries(attrs))e.setAttribute(k,String(v));if(text!==undefined)e.textContent=text;return e;}
function row(parent,values){const tr=document.createElement('tr');for(const v of values){const td=document.createElement('td');td.textContent=String(v);tr.append(td);}$(parent).append(tr);}function stop(){if(timer!==null)clearInterval(timer);timer=null;$('play').textContent='Play';}
const g=data.graph,positions=g.columns.map((col,i)=>{const members=g.columns.map((c,j)=>c===col?j:-1).filter(j=>j>=0);return [35+930*col/(2*g.depth+1),25+310*(members.indexOf(i)+.5)/members.length];});
g.adjacency.forEach((neighbors,i)=>neighbors.filter(j=>j>i).forEach(j=>{const a=positions[i],b=positions[j];$('graph').append(el('line',{x1:a[0],y1:a[1],x2:b[0],y2:b[1],stroke:Math.min(g.columns[i],g.columns[j])===g.depth?'#a95abc':'#bacbdd','stroke-width':1}));}));
positions.forEach(([x,y],i)=>{const node=el('circle',{cx:x,cy:y,r:i===g.entrance||i===g.exit?9:5,fill:g.columns[i]<=g.depth?'#2859d6':'#d27a17',tabindex:0,role:'button','aria-label':'Vertex '+g.names[i]});node.append(el('title',{},'Hidden name '+g.names[i]));const inspect=()=>{$('node-info').textContent=`Hidden name ${g.names[i]}\nNeighbor names: ${g.adjacency[i].map(j=>g.names[j]).join(', ')}\nDegree ${g.adjacency[i].length}; column ${g.columns[i]} (revealed only for explanation)`;};node.onclick=inspect;node.onkeydown=e=>{if(e.key==='Enter'||e.key===' '){e.preventDefault();inspect();}};$('graph').append(node);});
$('graph-info').textContent=`Explicit reference instance: depth ${g.depth}, seed ${g.seed}, ${g.vertices} vertices. Entrance name ${g.names[g.entrance]}; exit name ${g.names[g.exit]}. Measured neighbor-query BFS uses ${data.graph_bfs.neighbor_queries} queries. This drawing stays fixed when selecting larger reduced models.`;$('protocol').textContent=data.protocol;
for(const [i,r] of data.results.entries()){const option=document.createElement('option');option.value=i;option.textContent=r.depth;$('depth').append(option);row('sweep',[r.depth,r.vertices.toLocaleString(),r.columns,pct(r.uniform_quantum_exit),pct(r.uniform_classical_exit),(r.uniform_quantum_exit/r.uniform_classical_exit).toPrecision(4)+'×']);row('timings',[r.depth,r.timing.median_ms.quantum_ms.toFixed(4),r.timing.median_ms.classical_ms.toFixed(4)]);}
for(const v of data.verification)row('verification',[v.depth,v.vertices,v.invariance_max_error.toExponential(2),v.state_max_error.toExponential(2),v.bfs.neighbor_queries]);
function bars(id,values,color){const svg=$(id),r=data.results[index],slot=490/values.length;svg.replaceChildren();svg.append(el('line',{x1:40,x2:535,y1:200,y2:200,stroke:'#aabbd0'}));svg.append(el('text',{x:1,y:25,'font-size':11},'100%'));svg.append(el('text',{x:1,y:202,'font-size':11},'0%'));values.forEach((p,j)=>{const rect=el('rect',{x:40+j*slot,y:200-175*p,width:Math.max(.5,slot-2),height:Math.max(0,175*p),fill:color,stroke:j===column?'#d29216':'none','stroke-width':2,role:'button',tabindex:0,'aria-label':`Column ${j}, ${pct(p)}`});rect.append(el('title',{},`Column ${j}, ${r.column_sizes[j]} vertices, ${pct(p)} total`));rect.onclick=()=>{column=j;render();};rect.onkeydown=e=>{if(e.key==='Enter'||e.key===' '){e.preventDefault();column=j;render();}};svg.append(rect);});svg.append(el('text',{x:40,y:225,'font-size':11},'Entrance'));svg.append(el('text',{x:490,y:225,'font-size':11},'Exit'));}
function exitChart(r){const svg=$('exit-chart');svg.replaceChildren();for(const [y,label] of [[20,'100%'],[120,'50%'],[220,'0%']]){svg.append(el('line',{x1:50,x2:875,y1:y,y2:y,stroke:'#dce4ee'}));svg.append(el('text',{x:2,y:y+5,'font-size':12},label));}for(const [values,color] of [[r.quantum_probabilities,'#2859d6'],[r.classical_probabilities,'#d27a17']])svg.append(el('polyline',{points:values.map((v,i)=>`${50+825*r.times[i]/r.horizon},${220-200*v.at(-1)}`).join(' '),fill:'none',stroke:color,'stroke-width':3}));svg.append(el('text',{x:50,y:250,'font-size':12},'0'));svg.append(el('text',{x:710,y:250,'font-size':12},`Model time T=${r.horizon}`));}
function render(){const r=data.results[index],q=r.quantum_probabilities[frame],c=r.classical_probabilities[frame];$('time-range').value=frame;$('time').textContent=`t = ${r.times[frame].toFixed(3)} / ${r.horizon}`;$('back').disabled=frame===0;$('next').disabled=frame===r.times.length-1;bars('qbars',q,'#2859d6');bars('cbars',c,'#d27a17');const a=r.real[frame][column],b=r.imag[frame][column];let neighborReal=0,neighborImag=0;for(const j of [column-1,column+1])if(j>=0&&j<r.columns){const coupling=Math.min(j,column)===r.depth?Math.SQRT2:1;neighborReal+=coupling*r.real[frame][j];neighborImag+=coupling*r.imag[frame][j];}
$('amplitude').textContent=`Column ${column}: ${r.column_sizes[column].toLocaleString()} vertices\nAmplitude a = ${a.toFixed(6)} ${b<0?'−':'+'} ${Math.abs(b).toFixed(6)}i\nColumn probability |a|² = ${pct(q[column])}\nProbability per vertex = ${pct(q[column]/r.column_sizes[column])}\nWeighted neighbor-amplitude sum = ${neighborReal.toFixed(6)} + (${neighborImag.toFixed(6)})i\nda/dt = −i × neighbor sum = ${neighborImag.toFixed(6)} + (${(-neighborReal).toFixed(6)})i\nClassical column mass = ${pct(c[column])}`;
$('exit-info').textContent=`At t=${r.times[frame].toFixed(3)}: quantum ${pct(q.at(-1))}; classical ${pct(c.at(-1))}. Uniform-time averages over [0,${r.horizon}]: quantum ${pct(r.uniform_quantum_exit)}, classical ${pct(r.uniform_classical_exit)}. Descriptive sampled peak: ${pct(r.sampled_peak.probability)} at t=${r.sampled_peak.time.toFixed(3)}.`;}
function apply(){const r=data.results[index];frame=0;column=0;$('vertices').textContent=r.vertices.toLocaleString();$('columns').textContent=r.columns;$('register').textContent=r.column_qubits;$('encoding').textContent=`A compact full-vertex encoding would need ${r.compact_vertex_qubits} qubits; the theorem's opaque names use ${r.hidden_name_bits} bits (plus oracle workspace). Those registers are not simulated here. ${r.horizon_rule}.`;$('time-range').max=r.times.length-1;$('circuit').innerHTML=r.circuit_svg;$('timing-scope').textContent=`Five measured wall-time runs after two warmups per model. Python ${r.timing.python}, NumPy ${r.timing.numpy}, ${r.timing.platform}. ${r.timing.scope}`;exitChart(r);render();}
const scale=$('scaling'),times=data.results.flatMap(r=>[r.classical_mean_first_hit,r.quantum_ideal_repeat_evolution_time]),low=Math.floor(Math.log10(Math.min(...times))),high=Math.ceil(Math.log10(Math.max(...times))),maxDepth=Math.max(...data.results.map(r=>r.depth)),minDepth=Math.min(...data.results.map(r=>r.depth));for(let exponent=low;exponent<=high;exponent++){const y=235-200*(exponent-low)/Math.max(1,high-low);scale.append(el('line',{x1:65,x2:875,y1:y,y2:y,stroke:'#dce4ee'}));scale.append(el('text',{x:2,y:y+4,'font-size':11},'10^'+exponent));}for(const [key,color] of [['quantum_ideal_repeat_evolution_time','#2859d6'],['classical_mean_first_hit','#d27a17']])scale.append(el('polyline',{points:data.results.map(r=>`${65+810*(r.depth-minDepth)/Math.max(1,maxDepth-minDepth)},${235-200*(Math.log10(r[key])-low)/Math.max(1,high-low)}`).join(' '),fill:'none',stroke:color,'stroke-width':3}));scale.append(el('text',{x:65,y:265,'font-size':12},`Depth ${minDepth}`));scale.append(el('text',{x:800,y:265,'font-size':12},`Depth ${maxDepth}`));
$('depth').onchange=()=>{stop();index=Number($('depth').value);apply();};$('time-range').oninput=()=>{stop();frame=Number($('time-range').value);render();};$('back').onclick=()=>{stop();frame=Math.max(0,frame-1);render();};$('next').onclick=()=>{stop();frame=Math.min(data.results[index].times.length-1,frame+1);render();};$('play').onclick=()=>{if(timer!==null){stop();return;}if(frame===data.results[index].times.length-1)frame=0;$('play').textContent='Pause';timer=setInterval(()=>{frame++;render();if(frame===data.results[index].times.length-1)stop();},120);};apply();window.gluedLesson={data,get index(){return index;},get frame(){return frame;}};
</script></body></html>'''
